{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789036595,"nonce":"wB0LhaZAujEgfOQuY2J9zTfSSkY=","title":"The constants blob measures a pointer as 8 bytes in one pass and pointer-width in the other","message":"`emit_constants` in crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_data.rs walks the same constants\ntwice: pass 1 builds the member TYPES, pass 2 builds the member VALUES, and\nboth advance a cursor to decide where padding goes.\n\nFor HBYTES and HTYPE, pass 1 advances by `self.target_abi.pointer_bytes()` and\npass 2 advances by a hardcoded 8. On every 64-bit target those agree, so this\nis invisible; on wasm32 a pointer is 4 and the two passes put the following\nfield at different offsets, which means the values no longer describe the type\nthey are an initializer for.\n\nFound while chasing b66e8a1. Changing pass 2 to `pointer_bytes()` did NOT fix\nthat abort, so the two are unrelated and this one has no known symptom -- which\nis why it is filed rather than fixed: the change moves the wasm32 constant\nlayout and deserves a conformance run of its own, not a drive-by.\n\nBoth sites are in the same match, a few lines apart, and the fix is to make\npass 2 read `self.target_abi.pointer_bytes() as i32` the way pass 1 does.","files":null}]}